  'reading.book': "Tehillim",
  'reading.haazinuTitle': "Haazinu song",
  'reading.haazinuLead': "In the books of our Rebbeim, in the name of the Maharal of Prague, it is brought as a segulah (a spiritual aid) that learning the “Song of Haazinu” by heart is a segulah for prosperity.",
  'reading.haazinuAbout': "Our Rebbeim emphasized the special merit of the “Song of Haazinu”, which contains the whole Torah. Among chassidim it was customary to try to learn this song by heart and review it from time to time. A sign of the central place of this chapter is that from it we derive the mitzvah of writing a Torah scroll, the final positive mitzvah that completes the 613 mitzvot.",
  'reading.haazinuMark': "Haazinu · Deuteronomy 32",
  'reading.whyH': "The power of Tehillim.",
  'reading.why': "King David asked that whoever reads Tehillim be rewarded like one who studies the deepest laws of the Torah (Midrash Tehillim 1:8). That is why the Previous Rebbe, Rabbi Yosef Yitzchak of Lubavitch, set the custom of reading a portion of Tehillim every day by the day of the month — the whole book each month.",
  'reading.zrizH': "Zerizut — eagerness.",
  'reading.zriz': "“The eager hurry to do a mitzvah” (Pesachim 4a): Avraham rose early in the morning to do G-d's will. And Pirkei Avot (5:20): “Be swift as a deer … to do the will of your Father in Heaven.”",
  'reading.soH': "So — fluent reading.",
  'reading.so': "We invite you to do the mitzvah of reading Tehillim with eagerness: the recording leads you through the text, the highlight shows the word, and you can raise the speed up to 3.5× — then the daily portion takes a few minutes and the whole book 102 minutes.",
